{-
   Folding a container into one value with `++` - `Core.yana`'s `Append` and `Monoid`, used.

   Four functions and no new class. Three of them take the parts as `[m]` - a *window*, which every
   contiguous container converts to at the call - so they are available on an `Array`, on a slice of
   one, and on anything else that reaches `convertSlice`.

   **A window and not `Chunked(c, m)`, which is the one place this file differs from `Reduce.yana`.**
   That file walks a container to *read* it and takes the protocol, so a container keeping its
   elements in pieces is covered. These four walk it to *accumulate*, and the reason is now cost
   rather than capability.

   Two things used to rule the protocol out and neither still does. `chunks` is an `iter fn`, so the
   loop body is a continuation and every outer local it touches is captured by reference, and
   `acc = acc ++ part` through one of those was refused outright - which cost an `exchange` per part
   and would have dragged `Monoid` into even `appendAll`, since an exchange needs a value to leave
   behind. That is allowed now wherever a value is written back before the borrow is released; see
   test/lib/BorrowUpdate.yana. And a body constrained by a class whose member is an `iter fn` was
   said to have no erased form - *"concat cannot be given an environment"*. It does; both shapes were
   compiled through the forced-generic build and both answer correctly.

   What is left is that a window is cheaper and nothing needs the generality. The loop is a `while`
   in this frame, the accumulator is an ordinary owned local, and no environment is built - where
   `chunks` costs a closure per fold that the generic build cannot specialize away. What it gives up
   is a container that is not contiguous, and there is not one. `Chunked` becomes the right shape
   here the day there is, and it is now a change of one loop rather than a change of what the
   language permits.

   **The accumulator is consumed and the parts are copied**, which is the whole cost model and is
   worth stating once here rather than at each function. `++` takes both operands by `->`, so the
   accumulator is handed on from step to step and its storage is reused - that is what makes a fold
   linear rather than quadratic. A container hands out *views* of what it holds, though, so a part
   has to be duplicated before it can be given away, and `Copy(m)` is that duplicate. It is not
   overhead this convention introduced: the bytes have to reach the result somehow, and a borrowing
   `++` would have copied both the part and the accumulator instead of only the part.

   A caller who owns the parts and does not want them afterwards writes the loop with `++` directly,
   where each part is moved rather than copied. That is a real difference for an `Array(String)` and
   no difference at all for an `Array(Int)`, whose copy is its bytes.

   `empty()` selects on the expected type, the way `fromInt` does, so the ascription on the
   accumulator is what says which instance a fold starts from.
-}

{-
   The fold with the seed given, which is what `Append` alone can state.

   No `Monoid` here, because nothing in this body needs an answer for "no parts": the seed *is* that
   answer, and a caller who has one has already made the decision the identity would have made. So
   this is the primitive and `concat` below is written in terms of it - which is also the honest
   place to point a type with no empty value, since a rope or a non-empty list can join into a seed
   and cannot produce one.

   `appendAll(builder, more)` continues building in the seed's own storage rather than starting a
   second one: the seed is `->`, and `let &->` is what binds a moved value to a name that may then be
   written through.
-}
pub fn (Chunked(c, m), Append(m), Copy(m)) appendAll(->seed: m, parts: c) -> m:
    let &->acc = seed

    for v in items(parts):
        acc = acc ++ copy(v)

    return acc

-- Every part joined, in order, starting from the identity. `concat(["ab", "cd"])` is `"abcd"`, and
-- an empty container answers `empty()` - which is the case that needs an identity at all, and the
-- reason `Monoid` is a class rather than a comment on `Append`.
pub fn (Chunked(c, m), Monoid(m), Copy(m)) concat(parts: c) -> m = appendAll(empty() :: m, parts)

{-
   The same, with a separator between adjacent parts and not around the outside.

   `n` parts take `n - 1` separators, so something has to distinguish "before every part" from
   "between them". A flag and not an index, because `items` yields elements and not positions -
   there is no `i` to compare against zero. A test on the accumulator's emptiness would need `Eq(m)`
   and would be wrong anyway, since a genuinely empty first part is not the same thing as no first
   part.

   The separator is borrowed and copied per gap, because it is used more than once and `++` consumes
   what it is given. A caller passing a literal pays that copy `n - 1` times, which is what joining
   with a separator costs anywhere that does not intern it.
-}
pub fn (Chunked(c, m), Monoid(m), Copy(m)) joinWith(parts: c, separator: m) -> m:
    let &->acc = empty() :: m
    let &first = True

    for v in items(parts):
        if first then first = False else acc = acc ++ copy(separator)
        acc = acc ++ copy(v)

    return acc

{-
   One value joined to itself `times` times - the operation a `Monoid` has that a bare `Append` does
   not, because zero repetitions is the identity.

   A negative count is zero repetitions rather than a check, on the same terms as `slice`'s clamping:
   there is nothing a negative number of copies could mean, and the total answer is the useful one.

   Linear and not the halving a general `stimes` uses. Doubling would be `log(n)` *joins* rather than
   `n`, but each join copies its right operand, so the bytes moved are the same either way and the
   accumulator's own storage is never copied under this convention. What halving would buy is fewer
   calls, and what it would cost is a copy of the accumulator at each step - which is the trade the
   consuming `++` exists to refuse.
-}
pub fn (Monoid(m), Copy(m)) repeated(value: m, times: Int) -> m:
    let &->acc = empty() :: m
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < times:
        acc = acc ++ copy(value)
        i = i + 1

    return acc
